.TH "PORTUALE\-VDB" "1" "Oct 2026" "Portuale @VERSION@" "Portuale"
.SH "NAME"
portuale\-vdb \- Convert, verify, inspect, reindex or mount the installed\-package database
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.TP
.B portuale vdb convert
\fB\-\-from\fR \fIKIND:PATH\fR \fB\-\-to\fR \fIKIND:PATH\fR [\fB\-\-force\fR]
.TP
.B portuale vdb verify
[\fB\-\-against\fR] \fIKIND:PATH\fR \fIKIND:PATH\fR
.TP
.B portuale vdb status
\fIKIND:PATH\fR
.TP
.B portuale vdb sweep
(\fB\-\-remove\fR \fICAT/PF\fR)... | \fB\-\-all\fR \fIKIND:PATH\fR
.TP
.B portuale vdb rebuild\-index
\fIKIND:PATH\fR
.TP
.B portuale vdb mount
[\fB\-\-foreground\fR] [\fB\-\-allow\-other\fR] \fIKIND:PATH\fR \fIMOUNTPOINT\fR
.TP
.B portuale vdb
\fB\-\-help\fR
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
The installed\-package database (the VDB, normally \fB/var/db/pkg\fR) can
be kept in more than one backend.  \fBportuale vdb\fR copies it from one
backend to another and checks that two backends hold the same data.
It works on any pair of backends, in either direction.
.LP
\fBconvert\fR copies every live entry, with the exact bytes, modes and
mtimes of its files and the mode and mtime of its directory, then the
\fBworld\fR and \fBworld_sets\fR files, the preserved\-libs registry, the
config\-protect memory and the merge counter.  Counters are preserved,
never renumbered.  The consolidated \fBmetadata\fR file is never created,
and a stale stamp stays stale; a valid one is stamped again for the new
directory.  Entries that are still being merged (\fB\-MERGING\-\fR) are
not copied; each one is listed on standard error.  A database
destination is written in one transaction, so a failed conversion leaves
it unchanged.
.LP
\fBverify\fR compares two backends without changing either: the set of
live entries; for each entry its files, bytes, modes, mtimes, directory
mode and mtime, and whether the \fBmetadata\fR stamp is valid; then the
world files, preserved libs, config memory and counter.  The differences
are listed, at most 100 lines.
.LP
\fBstatus\fR prints the backend, the path, the number of installed entries,
the generation, the counter, the import mark of a database converted from
files, and the entries left mid\-merge by an interrupted merge (\fBmerging\fR
rows on a database, \fB\-MERGING\-\fR directories on files).  \fBmrg\fR
reports the same entries at startup on a database backend.
.LP
\fBsweep\fR deletes pending entries in one write transaction: the named ones
(\fB\-\-remove\fR, repeatable) or all of them (\fB\-\-all\fR).  A key that is
not pending, in particular an installed entry, refuses the whole sweep and
nothing is deleted.  Nothing is ever swept automatically.
.LP
\fBrebuild\-index\fR recomputes the index a \fBsqlite\fR or \fBredb\fR
database derives from the stored entry files: the file owners from
\fBCONTENTS\fR, the dependency atoms from the five \fB*DEPEND\fR files and
the lines of \fBNEEDED.ELF.2\fR.  File collision checks and reverse
dependency lookups read that index instead of the files.  Every installed
entry is reindexed in one write transaction, and the row counts are printed.
Run it once on a database converted by an older portuale, or when the index
is suspected to be wrong.  The entry files and the counter are not touched.
A \fBfiles:\fR tree has no index, so it is refused.
.LP
\fBmount\fR serves the database read\-only at \fIMOUNTPOINT\fR, an existing
directory, as the historic tree \fICAT\fR/\fIPF\fR/\fIfiles\fR, so tools
that read \fB/var/db/pkg\fR (eix, qlist, equery, real \fBportageq\fR and
\fBemerge \-p\fR) can use a database backend.  It uses FUSE through
\fBfusermount3\fR and needs \fB/dev/fuse\fR; no libfuse is linked, and it
exists only when portuale is built with the \fBvdb\-fuse\fR feature.
The database is opened read\-only.  Every operation that would change the
tree (create, write, unlink, rename, mkdir, symlink, link, chmod, truncate,
extended attributes) fails with \fBEROFS\fR.  File modes, sizes and mtimes
are the stored ones.  Directory mtimes follow real Portage (bug #290428):
an entry directory shows its stored mtime, a category directory the latest
mtime of its entries, and the root the latest of its categories.  The
consolidated \fBmetadata\fR file is served with its \fB#dir_mtime=\fR
stamp equal to the entry directory mtime shown, so real Portage accepts it;
a stamp that was stale when stored stays stale, and an entry stored without
a \fBmetadata\fR file has none.  Each directory listing and each open file
serves the generation it was opened on: a tool never sees half a merge, and
a file larger than 64 KiB that is rewritten while it is open reads as
\fBESTALE\fR instead of mixing old and new bytes.  A new lookup sees the
newest generation.  Without \fB\-\-foreground\fR the command detaches once the
mount is ready; unmount with \fBfusermount3 \-u\fR \fIMOUNTPOINT\fR.  Over
\fBsqlite\fR the mount can stay up while \fBmrg\fR merges.  Over \fBredb\fR the
mount holds the file read\-only: readers (\fBmrg \-\-pretend\fR, \fBverify\fR,
\fBstatus\fR) run beside it, while writers (a merging \fBmrg\fR,
\fBconvert\fR, \fBsweep\fR, \fBrebuild\-index\fR) fail as busy until it is
unmounted.  A
\fBfiles:\fR source is passed through unchanged, which is useful for
testing.  Real \fBemerge\fR and \fBebuild\fR merges do not work on a mount:
they must create lock files inside the tree.
.SH "KIND:PATH"
.TP
\fBfiles:\fR\fIROOT\fR
The historic tree: \fIROOT\fR\fB/var/db/pkg\fR plus the world,
preserved\-libs, config\-memory and counter files that live outside the
VDB directory, under \fIROOT\fR.  A \fIPATH\fR that ends in
\fBvar/db/pkg\fR means the root three levels up, so \fBfiles:/var/db/pkg\fR
is \fBfiles:/\fR.  As a source only, a directory without \fBvar/db/pkg\fR
is read as a bare VDB directory (entries only, no other stores).
.TP
\fBsqlite:\fR\fIFILE\fR
One SQLite file, created by \fBconvert\fR when missing.  Available only
when portuale is built with the \fBvdb\-sqlite\fR feature.
.TP
\fBredb:\fR\fIFILE\fR
One redb file, created by \fBconvert\fR when missing.  Available only when
portuale is built with the \fBvdb\-redb\fR feature.  redb allows one writer
process at a time: a command that writes to a file another process holds
open (a running \fBmrg\fR, another \fBvdb\fR command, a mount) fails at once
with "database is already open" (exit 2) instead of waiting.  Readers share
the file with each other, but also need it free of a writer.
.SH "OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB\-\-from\fR \fIKIND:PATH\fR
The source of \fBconvert\fR.  It is only read.
.TP
\fB\-\-to\fR \fIKIND:PATH\fR
The destination of \fBconvert\fR.  It must hold no entries, unless
\fB\-\-force\fR is given.
.TP
.B \-\-force
Make the destination an exact copy of the source: replace entries with
the same name and delete the ones the source does not have.
.TP
\fB\-\-remove\fR \fICAT/PF\fR
A pending entry for \fBsweep\fR to delete.  May be repeated.
.TP
.B \-\-all
\fBsweep\fR every pending entry.
.TP
.B \-\-foreground, \-f
Keep \fBmount\fR in the foreground.  SIGINT, SIGTERM and SIGHUP unmount
and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-other
Let other users, root in particular, read the mount.  Unless run by root
it needs \fBuser_allow_other\fR in \fB/etc/fuse.conf\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-against\fR
Accepted before the two arguments of \fBverify\fR.  It changes nothing.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
The conversion finished, \fBverify\fR found no difference, \fBstatus\fR
found nothing pending, or \fBsweep\fR or \fBrebuild\-index\fR finished.
.TP
.B 1
\fBverify\fR found differences, or \fBstatus\fR found pending entries.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error, or an I/O or database error (including a destination that
is not empty, a busy redb file, or \fBrebuild\-index\fR on \fBfiles:\fR).
.SH "EXAMPLES"
.nf
portuale vdb convert \-\-from files:/ \-\-to sqlite:/var/lib/portage/vdb.sqlite
portuale vdb verify \-\-against files:/ sqlite:/var/lib/portage/vdb.sqlite
portuale vdb convert \-\-from sqlite:vdb.sqlite \-\-to files:/tmp/vdb\-export
portuale vdb status sqlite:/var/lib/portage/vdb.sqlite
portuale vdb sweep \-\-remove dev\-libs/foo\-1.0 sqlite:/var/lib/portage/vdb.sqlite
portuale vdb rebuild\-index redb:/var/lib/portage/vdb.redb
portuale vdb mount \-\-allow\-other sqlite:/var/lib/portage/vdb.sqlite /mnt/vdb
fusermount3 \-u /mnt/vdb
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
\fBemerge\fR(1), \fBebuild\fR(1)
